// ROUND 124 -- PRISM'S ESCORT, in the prologue.
//
// She keeps a shorter leash than a party member (72) because the sewer is a
// corridor and the fog is tight: at the party distance she is regularly on the
// wrong side of a corner and reads as lost rather than as following.
const PRISM_FOLLOW_DIST = 52;
// ROUND 133 -- THE FLEEING IS GONE, AND SO ARE ITS THREE CONSTANTS.
//
// `PRISM_FLEE_RANGE`, `PRISM_FLEE_SPEED` and `PRISM_FLEE_BEARINGS` were the
// machinery of "she runs from any monster": how near one had to be, how much
// faster she ran than she followed, and nineteen bearings to try because in a
// corridor most of them are a wall. The user asked for none of it -- "Prism
// doesn't need to run away from enemies or the player, she can just follow the
// player like a summons would" -- and deleting the constants with the code is
// the point: a tuning number left behind for a behaviour that no longer exists
// is the next person's half-hour.
//
// How long she is allowed to make no headway toward the player before she
// simply arrives. Long enough that walking round a corner is not a teleport,
// short enough that the player never turns around to an empty corridor. This
// one stays: it is about a straight-line walker in a maze, not about fear.
const PRISM_STUCK_SECONDS = 2.4;
// How near a monster has to be before she says something about it. This is the
// old flee range kept for the one job that survived round 133: she notices
// things, and being frightened out loud is her character. It moves nothing.
const PRISM_NOTICE_RANGE = 190;

// ROUND 121 -- how long the weapon stays lit after an imbued strike. Long
// enough to read as the blow's own flourish at 60fps, short enough that it is
// never mistaken for a buff the player is carrying around -- which is exactly
// what it used to be, and what this round took out.
const IMBUE_GLOW_SECONDS = 0.5;

// ROUND 121 -- THE PERSON WHO TEACHES YOU TO PUT IT AWAY.
//
//   "A simple training in the adventurers guild should suffice. Actually it's
//    implied that projecting your aura all the time is considered sloppy and
//    second rate in the lore, so perhaps have NPCs note things like 'Apparently
//    (playername) doesn't understand how to retract their aura, guess they
//    haven't trained with (insert Aura training NPC name)' and other similar
//    hints."
//
// So the trainer needs a NAME the barks can use, and it has to be the same
// name in the guild hall, in the refusal when the button does not work, and in
// every needling line an NPC says -- which is why it is a constant rather than
// three copies of a string that would drift the first time one was reworded.
const AURA_TRAINER_NAME = 'Aura-Adept Serevin Kade';
// What the button says when you press it and cannot do it. This is where most
// players will learn the skill exists at all, so it names the person and the
// place, and it says WHY it matters rather than only that it is possible.
const AURA_UNTRAINED_TEXT =
  "Your aura is hanging out where anyone can read it -- rank, element, temper, "
  + "the lot. Everything with eyes knows what walked in.\n\n"
  + `Pulling it in is a trick, and nobody has taught it to you. ${AURA_TRAINER_NAME} `
  + 'keeps a corner of the Society hall for exactly this.';
// What he says once he has. Kept short: the training IS the conversation.
const AURA_TRAINED_TEXT =
  "Right. Stop pushing. That is the whole of it -- an aura leaks because you "
  + "are holding it open, and you have been holding it open since the day you "
  + "awakened because nobody told you that you were.\n\n"
  + "Pull it in. Feel where it sits against the skin. That is retracted: no "
  + "reach, no warning to anything in the room, and everything it does for YOU "
  + "still doing it. Push it back out when you want the room to know.\n\n"
  + "D-pad down, or R. Go and be less obvious.";
// ROUND 121 -- and the needling. The user asked for NPCs to note it, and the
// lore reason is the one they gave: projecting all the time is sloppy, so the
// people who say so are the ones who would notice -- other adventurers, guild
// staff, and anyone who has met a professional. One line per speaker rather
// than a pool of interchangeable ones, because the joke is that they each
// think they are the first to mention it.
const AURA_SLOPPY_BARKS = [
  `Apparently {name} hasn't worked out how to retract their aura. Guess they never trained with ${AURA_TRAINER_NAME}.`,
  "You can feel {name} coming from the end of the street. Some of us consider that a tell rather than an entrance.",
  `Aura out, all day, every day. ${AURA_TRAINER_NAME} would have something to say about that, and it would be short.`,
  "No offence meant, but leaving it projected like that is how a first-year gets remembered. Rarely fondly.",
  `{name} walks in lit up like a festival. Ask ${AURA_TRAINER_NAME} about it -- he's fixed worse.`,
  "There's a knack to pulling it in. Nobody's born with it. Most of us were told; somebody clearly forgot to tell you.",
];
// How often a passing NPC will mention it, at most, in seconds -- so the joke
// lands and then leaves the player alone.
const AURA_BARK_COOLDOWN = 90;

// ROUND 121 -- WHAT A RETRACTED AURA LOOKS LIKE.
//
//   "Aura retraction, the aura becomes a faint glow (of the appropriate color)
//    around the player model."
//
// A fraction of the ring's own scale and a fraction of its own alpha, so the
// glow is the same art in the same colour pulled in against the body rather
// than a second sprite that has to be kept in step with the first. 0.16 of a
// 110-radius field is about 18 units, which reads as a rim on the player at
// the game's zoom; 0.45 of an already-0.55 ring is faint without being gone.
const AURA_RETRACT_SCALE = 0.16;
const AURA_RETRACT_ALPHA = 0.45;
// How far a monster can be from a projected field's edge and still be judged
// by it. The user asked for the reaction to happen "Once on entering range",
// so the entry test is the aura's own radius; this is the slack that stops a
// creature pacing the boundary from being re-judged every second step.
const AURA_SENSE_HYSTERESIS = 36;
// The rank gap at which a projected aura frightens rather than provokes.
// "Monsters and foes should be able to sense a projected aura and will either
// run from it (If it's a rank or 2 higher than them) or attack it if it's the
// same or lower ranks" -- so one rank up is already enough to run.
const AURA_SENSE_FLEE_GAP = 1;
// How long a creature that decided to run keeps running. Long enough to clear
// a gold field's 214-unit radius at a walk, short enough that a fled pack is
// not permanently deleted from the encounter.
const AURA_FLEE_SECONDS = 4.5;

// =============================================================================
// ROUND 48 -- THE LEVER RUNTIME.  ***THE FIELD CONTRACT LIVES HERE.***
//
// essenceLevers.js gives the generator a mechanical vocabulary; five of those
// levers had no runtime at all, so a spec that carried them was inert. This
// block is the whole runtime, and this comment is the AUTHORITATIVE list of the
// spec fields it reads. The generator (awakening.js) must emit exactly these
// names -- nothing here inspects an ability's prose.
//
// Everything read off a spec is an OWN ENUMERABLE property, because round 47's
// _scaledAbility memo hands the cast path a `{ ...a }` CLONE. Nested objects
// (allyGrant, perAllyGain) survive that clone by reference, which is fine: they
// are read, never written.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. REACH -- "attacks and spells have double range for 45 seconds"
//      template: 'rangeBuff'      kind: 'active'
//      rangeMult      number   multiplier on reach (2 = double). default 2
//      buffDuration   number   seconds (SCALED_FIELDS already scales this)
//      cooldown, name, desc, color as any other active
//    Runtime: player.buffs.reach = { mult, t }. _reachMult() is read at the ONE
//    place _doPlayerAttack computes `range` (so hit test, damage loop and the
//    translucent telegraph all move together -- round 47's invariant), and
//    _reachScaled() multiplies the range-ish fields of any ability at cast.
//
// 2. CHAIN -- "make a weapon attack and on contact fire a fireball at a
//    DIFFERENT nearby enemy"
//      template: 'chainStrike'    kind: 'active'   (a strike that chains)
//      template: 'chainOnStrike'  kind: 'passive'  (every weapon swing chains)
//      base            number  the strike's own damage (scales)
//      range           number  reach of the opening strike (chainStrike only)
//      chainCount      number  how many OTHER enemies get hit. default 1
//      chainRange      number  how far from the STRUCK enemy to look. default 180
//      chainDamage     number  flat damage per chained bolt, OR...
//      chainDamagePct  number  ...a fraction of `base` (default 0.6). Preferred:
//                              it inherits `base`'s essence-level scaling.
//      element         string  the stone's channel (see 6)
//    Runtime: _fireChain(). The struck enemy and every enemy already chained to
//    are excluded, so a chain never doubles back.
//
// 3. ALLIES -- "grant allies within your aura +1 power and 15% increased fire
//    damage; each ally within your aura grants you +10% fire resistance and
//    +10% chance to inflict a burn with fire damage"
//      template: 'aura'  (the EXISTING passive aura, with two new blocks)
//      allyGrant   { power: number, dmgPct: number }   -- what each ally INSIDE gains
//      perAllyGain { resistPct: number, dotChancePct: number, dmgPct: number }
//                                                      -- what the BEARER gains,
//                                                         multiplied by the ally count
//      element     string  which channel the resistance and the DoT belong to
//    Runtime: counted on the aura's EXISTING tick (_tickAllyAura), never per
//    frame and never per ally. The count is stashed on the aura object itself,
//    so two ally-auras do not overwrite one another.
//
// 4. TURN -- a confused enemy fights whatever enemy stands nearest to it
//      template: 'confuseTurn'    kind: 'active'
//      range             number  radius around the player. default 140
//      duration          number  seconds (scales); `confuseDuration` also read
//      maxTargets        number  cap on how many are turned. default 99
//      confuseDamagePct  number  fraction of its own damage it deals. default 1
//    Runtime: m.confusedT. A confused monster skips the player-facing state
//    machine entirely (the same shape as the round-6 freeze) and runs
//    _updateConfusedMonster instead. Its kills are uncredited, exactly like a
//    guard's or a companion's.
//
// 5. FATE -- a second chance at a roll, and you can SEE it happen
//      template: 'fateReroll'     kind: 'passive'
//      rerollKinds     string[]  any of ['crit','dodge']. default both
//      rerollCooldown  number    seconds between rerolls, per kind. default 8
//    Runtime: a failed crit roll or a failed dodge roll is re-rolled once and
//    floats "Fate!" either way -- taken and missed is still information.
//    Counters in scene._fateStats for the suite.
//
// 6. ELEMENT -- the stone's material channel, on ANY spec
//      element  'fire'|'frost'|'lightning'|'nature'|'shadow'|'radiant'|'physical'
//    Runtime: threaded into _damageMonster's new 5th argument. A monster
//    RESISTS its own dmgElement and is VULNERABLE to the opposite one -- which
//    is the pairing round 38's weakness-sight overlay already draws over its
//    head, so the mark finally means something numeric. A typed ability also
//    stops being answered by armour, which is what "resisted as fire" means.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 7. TAUNT (ROUND 49) -- "Taunts (Drawing monsters to the tank and away from
//    the team)"
//      template: 'tauntPull'   kind: 'active'   category: 'defensive'
//      tauntRadius    number  how far the pull reaches. default 220
//      tauntDuration  number  seconds monsters stay fixed on the taunter. def 6
//      tauntMax       number  how many monsters it can hold. default 6
//      threatMult     number  damage multiplier while taunted (optional, >=1)
//      cooldown, name, desc, color as any other active
//    The same four fields are also read off ANY active carrying them, because
//    the `taunt` lever attaches them as a rider to a shield or an ironhide.
//    isTaunt() (awakening.js) is the single predicate for "does this taunt".
//
//    Runtime: m.taunt = { srcX, srcY, until, src, threatMult }, where `until`
//    is a time on the scene's COMBAT CLOCK (_tickCombatClock -- seconds,
//    advanced by the dt handed to the monster update) and not a wall-clock
//    timestamp. Same clock frozenT/confusedT/slowT run on, so a taunt stops
//    while the game does.
//      - srcX/srcY are refreshed from `src` every frame it holds, so a monster
//        drawn to a MOVING tank follows the tank rather than the tile the tank
//        was standing on when they shouted.
//      - while it holds, the chase branch aims at srcX/srcY instead of the
//        player, and the drop-aggro test is skipped entirely: walking away is
//        exactly what the taunt exists to stop working.
//      - `src` is the taunter: this.player, or a party member. _tauntSrcAlive()
//        drops the taunt when the source dies, goes down, un-recruits or is
//        left behind, which is the "and back to normal aggro" half.
//      - the pull takes the NEAREST tauntMax monsters, not everything in range.
//
//    THE COMPANION CASE IS THE FEATURE, not a bonus. "Drawing monsters to the
//    tank" only means something when the tank can be Benjamin and not the
//    player, so _applyTaunt takes a SOURCE rather than assuming the player, and
//    _partyCast routes a companion's taunt into the same function. A taunt that
//    only worked when the player cast it would be a self-peel, which is the one
//    thing the user's sentence does not describe.
// =============================================================================

const RANGE_BUFF_TEMPLATE = 'rangeBuff';
const CHAIN_STRIKE_TEMPLATE = 'chainStrike';
const CHAIN_PASSIVE_TEMPLATE = 'chainOnStrike';
const CONFUSE_TEMPLATE = 'confuseTurn';
const FATE_TEMPLATE = 'fateReroll';

// The ability fields a reach buff stretches. Deliberately NOT `radius` (that is
// a projectile's collision size, not its reach -- a fat bolt is not a long one;
// a bolt's reach is its flight `life`, handled at the spawn site) and NOT
// `auraRadius` (a standing field has a drawn ring, and a radius that no longer
// matched the ring would be a lie about where the field is).
// ROUND 49 -- tauntRadius joins them. Unlike auraRadius its ring is not a
// standing drawn field the player reads position off; it is a one-shot
// telegraph spawned at cast from this same number, so the ring and the pull
// move together and the buff cannot make the drawing lie.
const REACH_FIELDS = ['range', 'explodeRadius', 'freezeRadius', 'dashDist', 'teleportRange', 'chainRange', 'tauntRadius'];
// A sane ceiling. Double is the user's own example; four times a scythe's
// 270-degree sweep would be most of the screen.
const REACH_MULT_CAP = 3;

// The element pairing the perception overlay (_updatePerceptionOverlay) already
// draws: fire<->frost, lightning<->nature, shadow<->radiant.
const ELEMENT_OPPOSITE = {
  fire: 'frost', frost: 'fire', lightning: 'nature',
  nature: 'lightning', shadow: 'radiant', radiant: 'shadow',
};
// ROUND 74 -- the order a staff's element vote breaks ties in. Written out
// rather than derived from Object.keys(ELEMENT_OPPOSITE): key order is an
// implementation detail of whoever last edited that literal, and a build that
// threw frost on Monday and fire on Tuesday because two stones tied would look
// like a bug even though it is one.
const ELEMENT_VOTE_ORDER = ['fire', 'frost', 'lightning', 'nature', 'shadow', 'radiant'];
// ROUND 74 (item 8) -- what rank a god's armour set is made of. Silver, and
// level 9 within it: the disciple chain that grants it is reachable well before
// the end of the world, so making it Gold would put it out of reach of the
// player who just finished earning it. Its Divine rarity and its set bonus are
// what make it special; its RANK is what the equip gate reads.
const DIVINE_ARMOUR_RANK = 'silver';
// ROUND 74 -- the missile art's own resting heading, and how big to draw it.
//
// Every arrow, bolt and spear in the new sheets is drawn pointing UP AND TO
// THE RIGHT -- the same 45-degree diagonal the whole isometric art set uses --
// so a sprite rotated to its travel heading has to have that baked-in angle
// taken back off first. This is the same correction `pickFlightFx` carries as
// `baseAngle` for the spell bolts; it is a constant here because these sheets
// are one drop with one convention rather than a library with several.
const MISSILE_BASE_ANGLE = -Math.PI / 4;
// How long a missile should read ON SCREEN, in pixels before the camera's
// 1.25 zoom -- about one and a bit tiles, which is what makes an arrow legible
// against a 32px tile without looking like a thrown log.
//
// DERIVED PER SHEET rather than fixed, because the cells are not one size: the
// arrows and bolts are 24px and the spear bank is 64px, so a single scale
// factor draws one of them at nearly three times the other. Measured on a
// screenshot at a flat 0.85: the arrows came out as eight-pixel specks that
// were hard to see at all against the city paving.
const MISSILE_TARGET_PX = 34;
const missileScaleFor = (cell) => MISSILE_TARGET_PX / Math.max(1, cell);
// How much of a typed blow a monster's own element turns aside, and how much
// extra the opposite element lands for.
const MONSTER_ELEMENT_RESIST = 0.4;
const MONSTER_ELEMENT_WEAK = 0.4;
// The affliction each channel leaves behind, for the ally-aura's proc. Labels
// are chosen so statusKindForDot() routes each to the right status icon.
const ELEMENT_DOT_LABEL = {
  fire: 'Burn', frost: 'Frostbite', lightning: 'Shock',
  nature: 'Venom', shadow: 'Decay', radiant: 'Sear', physical: 'Bleed',
};
// A procced DoT is priced off the blow that caused it rather than off a
// constant, so it grows with the ability that lit it.
const ELEMENT_DOT_FRAC = 0.2;
const ELEMENT_DOT_TICKS = 4;
const ELEMENT_DOT_MS = 700;
// A companion has no attribute sheet, so an ally-aura's +power is paid to them
// in the currency they DO have: flat damage per point.
const ALLY_POWER_DMG = 2;
// Resistance is capped at 75% everywhere else in the game (stats.js CAPS); an
// ally aura must not be the one door around that.
const RESIST_CAP = 0.75;
const CONFUSE_TINT = 0xce93d8;
// How far a turned monster will look for someone else to fight.
const CONFUSE_SEEK_RANGE = 320;

// ROUND 49 (taunt lever) -- the pull's colour, and the leash.
//
// TAUNT_LEASH is the one number the field contract does not carry, and it has
// to exist: without it a monster taunted by a companion who is then teleported
// across the map (townPortal, a region change, _partyWalkTo's catch-up snap)
// would walk the whole world to reach them. Generous enough that it never
// interrupts a fight -- a taunt's own radius tops out near 260 -- and short
// enough that it cannot become a cross-region tow rope.
const TAUNT_TINT = 0xffb74d;
const TAUNT_COLOR = '#ffb74d';
// ROUND 49 -- every rollable buff stat, in BUFF_BASE's own order. Imported
// shape rather than a hand-typed list would be better still, but the list has
// to exist as an ORDER for the Team tab to print it in, and this is the order
// the player's own sheet uses.
const PARTY_GEAR_STATS = [
  'maxHp', 'maxStamina', 'maxMana', 'critChance', 'critDamage', 'blockChance',
  'dodgeChance', 'armor', 'hpRegen', 'staminaRegen', 'manaRegen', 'attackSpeed',
  'castSpeed', 'cooldownReduction', 'resist_fire', 'resist_frost',
  'resist_lightning', 'resist_nature', 'resist_shadow', 'resist_radiant',
];
/** The pools a companion has now, so maxStamina/maxMana and their regens have
 *  somewhere to land. Generous bases: the point of the stats is that gear moves
 *  them, not that a companion spends the fight empty. */
const PARTY_BASE_STAMINA = 100;
const PARTY_BASE_MANA = 120;
/** What one swing costs and what one cast costs. Low enough that an unequipped
 *  companion never starves, high enough that +max and +regen are felt. */
const PARTY_SWING_STAMINA = 8;
const PARTY_CAST_MANA = 14;
/** Base refill per second, before gear. Set so an unequipped companion can
 *  swing and cast indefinitely at their natural cadence -- the stats make them
 *  BETTER, they are not a tax on having none. */
const PARTY_STAMINA_REGEN = 9;
const PARTY_MANA_REGEN = 6;
// ROUND 49 -- STEALTH. Defaults for a spec that carries some of the lever's
// fields but not all of them (a rider on somebody else's ability), and the two
// colours the veil talks in.
const STEALTH_DEFAULT_DURATION = 7;
const STEALTH_DEFAULT_ALPHA = 0.35;
const STEALTH_DEFAULT_AGGRO = 0.4;
const STEALTH_COLOR = '#b39ddb';
const STEALTH_BREAK_COLOR = '#ff8a65';
/** How close you have to get before a lurking monster stands up. Deliberately
 *  short -- an ambush you can see coming from across the field is just a
 *  monster standing in the open. */
const LURK_TRIGGER = 120;
/** What an ambusher's opening swing is multiplied by. */
const AMBUSH_DAMAGE_MULT = 1.8;
/** What a lurking monster looks like. Semi transparent, per the user's word,
 *  and not invisible: a player who is looking should be able to spot one. */
// ROUND 49 -- "Make stealthed monsters even fainter." Was 0.34, which read as
// a monster standing in a light fog. At 0.16 a lurker is a suggestion in the
// grass: findable by a player who is looking for it, missed by one who is not,
// which is what an ambush is FOR. Still deliberately non-zero -- the user asked
// for "semi transparent", and a genuinely invisible monster is an unfair one.
const LURK_ALPHA = 0.16;
const TAUNT_LEASH = 900;

// Base spritesheet keys (loaded in preload) each real body type's recolor
// pipeline reads from -- see _applyAppearance.
const BODY_TYPE_BASE_KEYS = {
  m_muscular: { idle: 'playerIdle_m_muscular', attack: 'playerAttack_m_muscular', run: 'playerRun_m_muscular' },
  f_muscular: { idle: 'playerIdle_f_muscular', attack: 'playerAttack_f_muscular', run: 'playerRun_f_muscular' },
};

const GRASS_TILE_COLS = 4;
// v3 (round 4): each ground-tile atlas grew its own frame count this round
// (extract_round4_tilesets.py appended real crops from the 6 new tileset
// uploads onto each existing 16-frame atlas) -- grass/path/street no longer
// share one shared count. Each atlas is still saved as a 4-col grid, padded
// with transparent frames to a whole number of rows, so the REAL (non-
// padding) frame count below is what tileVariantHash's modulo must use --
// using the file's total grid-slot count instead would occasionally pick a
// blank transparent padding frame.
const GRASS_TILE_COUNT = 25, PATH_TILE_COUNT = 25, STREET_TILE_COUNT = 22;
// ROUND 19 -- "Remove the old city tiles and old grass tiles and only use
// the tilesets uploaded most recently."
//
// extract_round4_tilesets.py EXTENDED each atlas rather than replacing it:
// frames 0-15 of every atlas are the round-3 originals, and everything from
// frame 16 up is warped out of the round-4 tileset uploads. So "only use the
// most recent" is a floor on the variant index, not a new file -- the old
// frames stay in the atlas (harmless, and re-cutting the files would break
// every index in this module) but nothing selects them any more.
//
// Path tiles are deliberately NOT floored: the user named the city and grass
// sets, and the cardinal dirt paths read fine as they are.
const LEGACY_TILE_FRAMES = 16;
// The modern frames arrive in tonal GROUPS, and that matters for how they
// are picked. Grass 16-18 is pale flowering meadow, 19-21 bright green,
// 22-24 dark green; street 16-18 is grey cobble, 19-21 bright red brick.
// Choosing uniformly per tile put a light tile next to a dark one on every
// edge and the ground read as a checkerboard. So the GROUP is chosen from a
// low-frequency hash (a ~6-tile patch shares one group) and the variant
// WITHIN the group from the per-tile hash -- patches of consistent tone with
// real variety inside them, which is what the source textures look like.
// ROUND 101 -- THE TEXTURED GRASS, not the flat diamonds.
//
// These were frames 16-24, the tiles of `grassTileAtlas` that are exactly 50%
// opaque -- the diamond clipped to its own outline with no overhang. They are
// not literally one colour (6 to 14 of them each, checked, because "flat" was
// worth being right about) but they are speckle noise at one hue and one
// value: no flowers, no dirt edge, no silhouette. At play zoom that averages
// to a field of green. They were what the game had left after round 78
// eliminated 1-16, and they are 35% of every ground tile in the world.
//
// Frames 0-15 are the tufted grass: flowers, dirt edges, and 75-88% coverage,
// which is a full diamond PLUS the overhang that makes grass read as grass.
// Grouped four ways by row, because the rows are the variants that belong
// together and `patchPick` patches within a group.
//
// Four frames are held out of open ground, and NEITHER of the two guesses I
// made about which four survived being looked at:
//
//   3 and 7    the riverbank. Measured: 4.2% and 7.4% blue pixels against
//              0.0-0.1% for every other frame on the sheet, and the contact
//              sheet shows blue dashes and a bare dirt crossing. They are the
//              `bank` role in tilePlan.js, not open country.
//   2, 6, 10   the ones that CORDUROY. Each carries dark tufts at the same
//   and 14     place in the diamond, so tiled they line up into continuous
//              diagonal streaks -- a ploughed field, visible in the first
//              play-zoom shot of this round and reproduced by tiling each
//              frame alone in a 10x10 block and looking at the sixteen
//              swatches side by side.
//
// The first draft dropped 3, 7, 11 and 15 -- the whole of the fourth column --
// on the theory that "the water frames are a column". 11 and 15 are ordinary
// grass and the streaking column was the third one. A tile sheet's columns
// mean whatever the artist meant by them; the only way to know is to tile each
// frame by itself and look at it, which takes about a minute.
const GRASS_TILE_GROUPS = [[0, 1], [4, 5], [8, 9, 11], [12, 13, 15]];
// Streets and the plaza do NOT patch. Grey cobble (16-18) and red brick
// (19-21) differ in hue rather than tone, so patching them produced large
// solid red blocks across the town square -- it read as a hazard, not as
// paving. They are picked per TILE instead, weighted 7:1 toward grey, which
// scatters the red as isolated inlay stones the way real decorative paving
// works.
// ROUND 37 -- the city paving is real tile art now. "The previous city tiles
// can be replaced by the brick pavers and road pavement stone for red and
// grey respectively." Both packs measured natively 64x32, so the diamonds
// are cropped straight out with NO resampling -- which is the round's whole
// rule. 16 designs each, up from 3, so the streets stop repeating.
const STREET_GREY = CITY_GREY_FRAMES, STREET_RED = CITY_RED_FRAMES;
// ROUND 80 (items 1.2, 1.3) -- WHICH MATERIAL EACH REGION'S CITY FLOOR IS
// PAVED FROM. Region 1 is absent on purpose: Cadence keeps the red brick and
// the herringbone it was built with, which nobody has asked to change.
const CITY_FLOOR_PLAN = {
  ontaria: 'harrowmoor_city_floor',   // "the grey city tile"
  elehyd: 'karsk_harrowmoor_base',    // 7.12's street tiles, unchanged
  bratugal: 'vashra_city_floor',      // "the red brick tile", full city floor
};
// The capitals whose whole disc is paved as city floor. Only the two the user
// named: Karsk Landing was not mentioned and keeps the gravel-and-street-tile
// mix round 78 gave it, and Cadence is already stencilled as city floor by
// `_stampCadence`.
// ROUND 100 -- Karsk Landing joins them at the user's word ("give it a city
// floor too"). It was the only capital with no floor of any kind: red
// badlands with a grey street cross laid over it, which read as a crossroads
// rather than as the last place west with walls.
const CITY_FULL_FLOOR = { ont_city: true, bra_city: true, ele_city: true };

// ===========================================================================
// ROUND 100 -- A ROAD IS A DIFFERENT COLOUR FROM THE GROUND IT CROSSES.
//
// THE USER: "add a tile of a different color as roads 2 tiles wide."
//
// The WIDTH was never the problem: STREET_TILES has been 2 since round 19 and
// `band()` measures it in whole tiles, so a road is exactly two tiles across
// everywhere. The problem is that nobody could see it. `_tileArtFor` consults
// the region's CITY_FLOOR_PLAN before it looks at the tile type, so TILE_CITY
// and TILE_STREET both came back as the same material in the same colour --
// two patterns of one brick. Vashra's screenshot is a single continuous apron
// with houses standing on it and no street visible anywhere in the frame.
//
// So a street gets its OWN sheet: the herringbone pack, palette-shifted to a
// pale grey flagstone through the same rampLut/lumaRange route the temple
// floors use. One material worldwide rather than one per region, because a
// road that is a different colour in every city is four road materials and no
// road IDENTITY -- and this pale neutral is the one value that reads against
// all four floors: Cadence's terracotta, Harrowmoor's grey-blue, Vashra's red
// brick and Karsk's red badlands.
//
// `lumaRange` matters here for the reason round 76 wrote it: without it the
// remap reads raw luminance and a mid-scale tile sheet only ever touches the
// middle of the ramp, which is how you get a flat grey smear instead of stone.
// Two passes of looking to get here, and both failures are worth keeping.
//
//   1. [0x1e2024 .. 0xeef1f5] -- a full-range ramp topping out near white. The
//      roads came back glittering, reading as snow rather than as stone.
//   2. [0x20242a .. 0xc2c9d2] -- still full-range, just dimmer. On Harrowmoor's
//      grey-blue floor the roads read as DARK smudges, because a ramp that
//      spans the whole scale gives a road as many dark pixels as light ones and
//      the eye averages them below the floor it crosses.
//
// A road is not a full-range material. It is a PALE one, so the ramp lives in
// the top half of the scale and its darkest stop is still lighter than every
// city floor in the game. The herringbone's own shading does the rest.
const ROAD_RAMP = [0x8b929b, 0xa1a8b1, 0xb4bbc4, 0xc5ccd4, 0xd5dbe2, 0xe7ebf0];
const ROAD_SHEET_KEY = 'cityStone~road';

// ROUND 100 -- the Society's own colour, and the shape of the ground it
// keeps outside its door. Gilt is the user's pick: "pale stone and gold",
// an institution that wants to be found. The forecourt is measured in tiles
// out from the doorstep, with GAP tiles left open in the middle of the front
// rail so there is a way in.
// Pale stone and gold, the user's pick, as a six-stop luminance ramp rather
// than a palette key -- see `_societyHallSheet` for why the palette route
// produced a hall indistinguishable from the houses beside it.
const SOCIETY_RAMP = [0x241b08, 0x4d3a12, 0x8a6c22, 0xc4a244, 0xe4cf86, 0xf7edcb];
// SMALL, and measured rather than chosen: the civic row stands its buildings
// about four tiles apart, so a forecourt three tiles to either side reaches
// into the neighbours' collision circles and most of its railing is refused.
// Five of thirteen panels landed at the first size. Two either side fits.
const SOCIETY_COURT_DEPTHS = [2, 3, 1, 4];  // tried in this order
const SOCIETY_COURT_HALF = 4;    // tiles either side
const SOCIETY_COURT_GAP = 0;     // tiles left open, centred on the door
// Which of `fence`'s eight frames draws a panel running along the world's
// +x axis. The sheet is a post and three panel angles, twice.
const SOCIETY_FENCE_FRAME = 1;

// ROUND 30 -- "Unlike the randomness of nature cobblestone is designed and
// therefore should look consistent and planned."
//
// Both pools stay, but nothing picks between them by hash any more. A road
// is ONE material (red brick) and the plaza is concentric rings alternating
// the two, both keyed off geometry rather than a scatter. The hash still
// chooses WITHIN a material -- three cuts of the same brick is texture
// variety, not a random material choice, and it stops a long avenue from
// stamping one identical tile a hundred times.
const PLAZA_RING_TILES = 2;   // ring width, in tiles
// ROUND 31 -- the attack telegraph: "flash for 8 frames in a light
// see-through red on the ground". Held for a frame COUNT rather than a
// duration so it is the same 8 rendered frames on any machine.
const SWING_FX_COLOR = 0xff4d4d;
const SWING_FX_ALPHA = 0.30;
const SWING_FX_FRAMES = 8;

// ROUND 68 -- THE MIX WITHIN A BUS.
//
// The sliders set what a bus is worth; these set what things are worth
// RELATIVE TO EACH OTHER inside one, and they exist because the four kinds of
// sound on the spells bus are not the same size of event.
//
